{"module":"incarceration","voice":"female","duration":230.5,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Mass incarceration and the war on drugs","start":0.0,"end":3.2,"words":[[0.05,0.36],[0.36,1.6],[1.6,1.91],[1.91,2.12],[2.12,2.33],[2.33,2.54],[2.54,3.05]]},{"el":"intro","text":"A 911 call can be the first step into the criminal legal system.","start":4.0,"end":8.33,"words":[[4.05,4.17],[4.17,5.29],[5.29,5.47],[5.47,5.64],[5.64,5.76],[5.76,5.88],[5.88,6.11],[6.11,6.35],[6.35,6.59],[6.59,6.7],[6.7,7.17],[7.17,7.47],[7.47,7.88]]},{"el":"intro","text":"This module shows how big that system became, who it falls on, and the laws behind it.","start":8.68,"end":15.34,"words":[[8.73,8.99],[8.99,9.49],[9.49,9.83],[9.83,10.09],[10.09,10.34],[10.34,10.59],[10.59,11.1],[11.1,11.69],[11.98,12.15],[12.15,12.32],[12.32,12.66],[12.66,12.91],[13.21,13.46],[13.46,13.63],[13.63,13.88],[13.88,14.47],[14.47,14.73]]},{"el":"h0","text":"The numbers","start":15.69,"end":16.73,"words":[[15.73,15.93],[15.93,16.56]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 1.9 million people are held in U.S. prisons, jails and detention centers (Prison Policy Initiative), up from about 300,000 in the early 1970s.","start":17.43,"end":29.77,"words":[[17.48,17.86],[17.86,18.76],[18.76,19.21],[19.21,19.59],[19.59,19.66],[19.66,19.96],[19.96,20.11],[20.11,20.49],[20.86,21.46],[21.73,22.18],[22.18,22.4],[22.4,23.0],[23.0,23.45],[23.45,23.9],[23.9,24.35],[24.35,25.11],[25.37,25.52],[25.52,25.82],[25.82,26.19],[26.19,27.47],[27.47,27.62],[27.62,27.77],[27.77,28.0],[28.0,29.2]]},{"el":"s0","text":"No country locks up a larger share of its people.","start":30.12,"end":33.0,"words":[[30.16,30.36],[30.36,30.76],[30.76,31.02],[31.02,31.16],[31.16,31.29],[31.29,31.62],[31.62,31.82],[31.82,31.95],[31.95,32.15],[32.15,32.55]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Black Americans are imprisoned in state prisons at nearly five times the rate of white Americans (Sentencing Project, 2021).","start":33.35,"end":41.69,"words":[[33.39,33.67],[33.67,34.3],[34.3,34.37],[34.37,35.0],[35.0,35.14],[35.14,35.49],[35.49,35.98],[35.98,36.12],[36.12,36.47],[36.47,36.75],[36.75,37.1],[37.1,37.24],[37.24,37.52],[37.52,37.66],[37.66,37.94],[37.94,38.57],[38.57,39.2],[39.2,39.83],[40.07,41.19]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In 2010, Pew found that about 1 in 9 Black children had a parent behind bars.","start":42.04,"end":47.94,"words":[[42.1,42.24],[42.24,42.95],[43.2,43.41],[43.41,43.76],[43.76,43.98],[43.98,44.33],[44.33,44.54],[44.54,44.69],[44.69,44.97],[44.97,45.25],[45.25,45.82],[45.82,46.03],[46.03,46.18],[46.18,46.67],[46.67,47.17],[47.17,47.45]]},{"el":"h1","text":"“Except as a punishment for crime”","start":48.84,"end":50.96,"words":[[48.89,49.33],[49.33,49.48],[49.48,49.63],[49.63,50.29],[50.29,50.44],[50.44,50.81]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The 13th Amendment's exception, which made convict leasing legal after slavery (see the module on policing), still allows forced labor in prisons.","start":51.66,"end":61.36,"words":[[51.71,51.86],[51.86,52.32],[52.32,53.09],[53.09,53.78],[54.05,54.36],[54.36,54.66],[54.66,55.2],[55.2,55.58],[55.58,55.97],[55.97,56.27],[56.27,56.89],[56.89,57.04],[57.04,57.19],[57.19,57.65],[57.65,57.81],[57.81,58.42],[58.69,59.0],[59.0,59.38],[59.38,59.69],[59.69,60.07],[60.07,60.22],[60.22,60.84]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Starting with Colorado in 2018, several states have removed similar exceptions from their own constitutions; others have voted to keep them.","start":61.71,"end":70.67,"words":[[61.74,62.15],[62.15,62.36],[62.36,62.99],[62.99,63.13],[63.13,64.04],[64.28,64.7],[64.7,65.12],[65.12,65.32],[65.32,65.74],[65.74,66.16],[66.16,66.79],[66.79,67.07],[67.07,67.28],[67.28,67.48],[67.48,68.46],[68.7,68.98],[68.98,69.19],[69.19,69.61],[69.61,69.75],[69.75,69.96],[69.96,70.24]]},{"el":"h2","text":"The war on drugs","start":71.57,"end":73.07,"words":[[71.62,71.86],[71.86,72.09],[72.09,72.33],[72.33,72.93]]},{"el":"s2","text":"President Nixon called drug abuse “public enemy number one” in 1971.","start":73.77,"end":79.0,"words":[[73.82,74.45],[74.45,74.88],[74.88,75.16],[75.16,75.44],[75.44,75.8],[75.8,76.22],[76.22,76.58],[76.58,76.93],[76.93,77.14],[77.14,77.29],[77.29,78.56]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Anti-Drug Abuse Act of 1986 set the same mandatory minimum sentence for 5 grams of crack as for 500 grams of powder cocaine, a 100-to-1 ratio that fell overwhelmingly on Black defendants.","start":79.35,"end":93.91,"words":[[79.39,79.55],[79.55,80.16],[80.16,80.54],[80.54,80.77],[80.77,80.93],[80.93,82.08],[82.08,82.31],[82.31,82.46],[82.46,82.77],[82.77,83.46],[83.46,83.99],[83.99,84.53],[84.53,84.68],[84.68,84.99],[84.99,85.37],[85.37,85.53],[85.53,85.83],[85.83,85.99],[85.99,86.14],[86.14,86.98],[86.98,87.37],[87.37,87.52],[87.52,87.9],[87.9,88.52],[88.79,88.94],[88.94,90.09],[90.09,90.7],[90.7,90.93],[90.93,91.16],[91.16,92.08],[92.08,92.24],[92.24,92.54],[92.54,93.39]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Congress cut the ratio to 18-to-1 in 2010 (Fair Sentencing Act) and made that retroactive in 2018 (First Step Act).","start":94.26,"end":102.75,"words":[[94.3,94.82],[94.82,95.04],[95.04,95.18],[95.18,95.77],[95.77,95.92],[95.92,96.65],[96.65,96.8],[96.8,97.46],[97.46,97.68],[97.68,98.34],[98.34,98.56],[98.82,99.04],[99.04,99.33],[99.33,99.55],[99.55,100.36],[100.36,100.5],[100.5,101.38],[101.38,101.68],[101.68,101.97],[101.97,102.27]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Black and white Americans use marijuana at similar rates, but in 2018 Black people were 3.6 times more likely to be arrested for possessing it (ACLU, 2020).","start":103.1,"end":115.02,"words":[[103.15,103.46],[103.46,103.7],[103.7,104.01],[104.01,104.71],[104.71,104.95],[104.95,105.57],[105.57,105.73],[105.73,106.2],[106.2,106.67],[106.94,107.17],[107.17,107.33],[107.33,108.27],[108.27,108.58],[108.58,108.97],[108.97,109.13],[109.13,110.06],[110.06,110.46],[110.46,110.61],[110.61,111.08],[111.08,111.24],[111.24,111.39],[111.39,111.94],[111.94,112.1],[112.1,112.64],[112.64,112.8],[112.8,113.19],[113.46,114.48]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Did the war on drugs aim at Black communities?","start":115.92,"end":118.6,"words":[[115.95,116.14],[116.14,116.27],[116.27,116.39],[116.39,116.52],[116.52,116.84],[116.84,117.03],[117.03,117.15],[117.15,117.4],[117.4,118.1]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In a 2016 Harper's article, writer Dan Baum quoted Nixon aide John Ehrlichman as saying, in a 1994 interview, that the administration linked Black people with heroin in order to disrupt their communities.","start":119.3,"end":133.98,"words":[[119.35,119.5],[119.5,119.65],[119.65,120.63],[120.63,121.01],[121.01,121.53],[121.79,122.17],[122.17,122.4],[122.4,122.7],[122.7,123.22],[123.22,123.67],[123.67,123.9],[123.9,124.2],[124.2,124.95],[124.95,125.1],[125.1,125.55],[125.81,125.96],[125.96,126.11],[126.11,127.24],[127.24,127.84],[128.1,128.33],[128.33,128.48],[128.48,129.53],[129.53,129.91],[129.91,130.21],[130.21,130.58],[130.58,130.81],[130.81,131.33],[131.33,131.48],[131.48,131.71],[131.71,131.86],[131.86,132.38],[132.38,132.61],[132.61,133.44]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Ehrlichman's family disputes the quote, and he can't confirm it (he died in 1999).","start":134.33,"end":140.38,"words":[[134.39,135.15],[135.15,135.57],[135.57,136.12],[136.12,136.26],[136.26,136.68],[136.92,137.13],[137.13,137.27],[137.27,137.55],[137.55,137.96],[137.96,138.1],[138.1,138.24],[138.24,138.52],[138.52,138.66],[138.66,139.91]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Historians agree the drug war's effects fell hardest on Black communities.","start":140.73,"end":145.28,"words":[[140.77,141.49],[141.49,141.78],[141.78,141.93],[141.93,142.22],[142.22,142.44],[142.44,142.87],[142.87,143.09],[143.09,143.53],[143.53,143.67],[143.67,143.96],[143.96,144.76]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Whether that was the goal from the start is debated.","start":145.63,"end":148.33,"words":[[145.69,145.93],[145.93,146.11],[146.11,146.29],[146.29,146.41],[146.41,146.65],[146.65,146.9],[146.9,147.02],[147.02,147.26],[147.26,147.38],[147.38,147.92]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The 1994 Crime Bill is debated too.","start":148.68,"end":152.04,"words":[[148.72,148.87],[148.87,150.0],[150.0,150.37],[150.37,150.6],[150.6,150.75],[150.75,151.35],[151.35,151.57]]},{"el":"s3","text":"It funded police and prisons and encouraged “three strikes” and truth-in-sentencing laws.","start":152.39,"end":158.51,"words":[[152.44,152.6],[152.6,153.1],[153.1,153.51],[153.51,153.76],[153.76,154.34],[154.34,154.58],[154.58,155.32],[155.32,155.57],[155.57,156.15],[156.15,156.4],[156.4,157.63],[157.63,157.96]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Some Black leaders and mayors supported it at the time because of real violence in their communities.","start":158.86,"end":164.65,"words":[[158.88,159.1],[159.1,159.39],[159.39,159.76],[159.76,159.98],[159.98,160.28],[160.28,160.79],[160.79,160.94],[160.94,161.09],[161.09,161.23],[161.23,161.53],[161.53,161.9],[161.9,162.04],[162.04,162.34],[162.34,162.93],[162.93,163.08],[163.08,163.3],[163.3,164.11]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Researchers still argue over how much it drove incarceration, since most prisoners are held under state laws.","start":165.0,"end":172.12,"words":[[165.06,165.67],[165.67,165.97],[165.97,166.28],[166.28,166.58],[166.58,166.81],[166.81,167.12],[167.12,167.27],[167.27,167.65],[167.65,168.64],[168.91,169.21],[169.21,169.59],[169.59,170.2],[170.2,170.28],[170.28,170.58],[170.58,170.89],[170.89,171.27],[171.27,171.57]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Punishment after the sentence","start":173.02,"end":174.98,"words":[[173.07,173.79],[173.79,174.11],[174.11,174.27],[174.27,174.83]]},{"el":"s4","text":"A record can follow someone for life: it can bar them from jobs, housing, student aid and, in many states, voting (see the module on the vote).","start":175.68,"end":185.0,"words":[[175.73,175.87],[175.87,176.22],[176.22,176.43],[176.43,176.78],[176.78,177.21],[177.21,177.35],[177.35,177.7],[177.94,178.08],[178.08,178.29],[178.29,178.43],[178.43,178.65],[178.65,178.93],[178.93,179.35],[179.59,180.09],[180.33,180.89],[180.89,181.1],[181.1,181.39],[181.63,181.77],[181.77,182.05],[182.05,182.55],[182.79,183.21],[183.21,183.35],[183.35,183.49],[183.49,183.92],[183.92,184.06],[184.06,184.2],[184.2,184.55]]},{"el":"s4","text":"“Ban the box” rules, first adopted by Hawaii in 1998, now delay criminal-history questions in many public and private hiring processes.","start":185.35,"end":195.26,"words":[[185.4,185.61],[185.61,185.75],[185.75,186.03],[186.03,186.39],[186.63,186.91],[186.91,187.41],[187.41,187.62],[187.62,188.04],[188.04,188.18],[188.18,189.44],[189.69,189.9],[189.9,190.25],[190.25,191.31],[191.31,191.94],[191.94,192.08],[192.08,192.36],[192.36,192.78],[192.78,192.99],[192.99,193.48],[193.48,193.98],[193.98,194.68]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Since Pennsylvania in 2018, a growing number of states have passed “Clean Slate” laws that automatically seal eligible old records.","start":195.61,"end":204.8,"words":[[195.66,195.97],[195.97,196.91],[196.91,197.06],[197.06,198.08],[198.35,198.51],[198.51,198.97],[198.97,199.37],[199.37,199.52],[199.52,199.99],[199.99,200.22],[200.22,200.54],[200.54,200.85],[200.85,201.24],[201.24,201.47],[201.47,201.71],[201.71,202.49],[202.49,202.72],[202.72,203.43],[203.43,203.74],[203.74,204.28]]},{"el":"h5","text":"What helps","start":205.7,"end":206.72,"words":[[205.74,206.04],[206.04,206.55]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Expungement clinics, Clean Slate laws, reentry programs, and alternatives to police for crisis calls (see the module on policing) all reduce the harm.","start":207.42,"end":218.34,"words":[[207.47,208.42],[208.42,209.06],[209.33,209.65],[209.65,210.05],[210.05,210.36],[210.64,211.27],[211.27,212.06],[212.34,212.58],[212.58,213.37],[213.37,213.53],[213.53,213.92],[213.92,214.08],[214.08,214.64],[214.64,214.95],[214.95,215.11],[215.11,215.27],[215.27,215.74],[215.74,215.9],[215.9,216.46],[216.73,216.89],[216.89,217.37],[217.37,217.53],[217.53,217.84]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Lawyers and paralegals can make a direct difference: many people eligible to clear a record never do because they don't know how.","start":218.69,"end":225.64,"words":[[218.74,219.05],[219.05,219.24],[219.24,219.86],[219.86,220.05],[220.05,220.3],[220.3,220.42],[220.42,220.86],[220.86,221.36],[221.58,221.83],[221.83,222.15],[222.15,222.71],[222.71,222.83],[222.83,223.08],[223.08,223.21],[223.21,223.52],[223.52,223.77],[223.77,223.9],[223.9,224.21],[224.21,224.4],[224.4,224.71],[224.71,224.9],[224.9,225.15]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The quest for this module asks you to help someone do it.","start":225.99,"end":229.58,"words":[[226.04,226.19],[226.19,226.55],[226.55,226.7],[226.7,226.92],[226.92,227.36],[227.36,227.65],[227.65,227.8],[227.8,227.95],[227.95,228.24],[228.24,228.68],[228.68,228.83],[228.83,229.05]]}]}